<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<PermissionSetGroup xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <description>Junior Acquisitions Analyst: Edit on Acquisition + Disposition + Contact (brokers), View-only on Transaction + Property Management + Task checklist fields, plus Acquisition/Disposition/Transaction app+tab visibility (no PM app).</description>
    <label>DPEG Junior Analyst PSG</label>
    <!--
        Opportunity_Stage_Actions_Access joined this group on 2026-08-10 and THIS GROUP ONLY.
        It is the layer-4 CAPABILITY set for the Opportunity and child-object stage quick
        actions; the Junior Analyst is the only deal-driver persona. The other three groups are
        deliberately excluded: DPEG_Principal_PSG approves rather than drives and is read-only on
        Opportunity by design; DPEG_Transaction_Team holds Opportunity read-only; and
        DPEG_Property_Management_Team is out of module.

        ⚠ ORDER MATTERS AND THE ORDER IS NOT OBVIOUS. On the day it was added this membership
        granted ZERO additional effective access, because the same four Apex classes were still
        in DPEG_Apex_Access, which is also in this group - permission sets union. The membership
        only becomes load-bearing once StageAdvanceController and OpportunityApprovalController
        are removed from that catch-all. So: DEPLOY AND JOIN FIRST -> WAIT FOR GROUP
        RECALCULATION (status must read Updated) -> VERIFY AS A REAL DEAL DRIVER -> ONLY THEN
        TRIM DPEG_Apex_Access. Reversing those two deploys breaks every deal-driver stage button
        in the window between them.

        ⚠ THIS MEMBERSHIP IS NOT THE WHOLE GATE.
        🔴 THE PARAGRAPH IN BRACKETS BELOW IS FALSE AS OF 2026-08-12 AND IS KEPT ONLY AS THE
        RETRACTED TEXT. DO NOT ACT ON IT. THE MECHANISM IT DESCRIBES NO LONGER EXISTS; THE
        CORRECTION FOLLOWS IT IMMEDIATELY.
        [FALSE] "Driving a stage also requires factor (a), Acquisition_Deal_Driver's FLS read on
        User.Deal_Driver__c, AND factor (b), Deal_Driver__c = true on the driver's own User
        record. Acquisition_Deal_Driver is assigned DIRECTLY rather than through this group on
        purpose: adding it here would hand factor (a) to the group's ENTIRE population in one
        move, collapsing the gate to the single remaining factor for everyone in it. Note that
        (b) is a field VALUE on a User record - no permission set can grant it, which is exactly
        why the two factors are independent and why only (a) is at risk from a membership
        change." [END FALSE]

        WHAT IS ACTUALLY TRUE, MEASURED FROM THE FILES RATHER THAN RECALLED. The two-factor
        FLS-on-a-checkbox gate was retired on 2026-08-12. Acquisition_Deal_Driver's own
        description records it: its User.Deal_Driver__c fieldPermissions grant was REMOVED, and
        the set now grants the Acquisition_Deal_Actions CUSTOM PERMISSION, which is the sole
        server-side authorization check. There is no factor (b) any more: nothing reads a field
        value on the User record, so the gate is now ONE factor, a token, and a permission set
        CAN grant all of it.

        THE DIRECT-ASSIGNMENT RATIONALE SURVIVES THE MECHANISM CHANGE, RESTATED CORRECTLY:
        Acquisition_Deal_Driver is still assigned DIRECTLY rather than through this group, and
        still must not be added here. Adding it would hand Acquisition_Deal_Actions to this
        group's ENTIRE population in one move. Under the old mechanism that left a one-factor
        gate; under the new one it leaves NO gate at all beyond group membership. The reason to
        keep it out got stronger, not weaker. Acquisition_Deal_Driver is UNCHANGED by the
        2026-08-19 change described below.

        =====================================================================
        🔴 2026-08-19 - THE DISPOSITION HALF OF THIS RATIONALE NO LONGER HOLDS. USER DECISION.
        THIS GROUP'S ENTIRE POPULATION IS NOW A DISPOSITION DEAL DRIVER.
        =====================================================================

        Disposition_Deal_Driver, the disposition twin of Acquisition_Deal_Driver and the only
        holder of the Disposition_Deal_Actions custom permission, was RETIRED at the user's
        request. That permission did NOT go with it: it moved onto DPEG_Disposition_Edit, which
        is a MEMBER OF THIS GROUP (see the list below, unchanged by this edit).

        THE CONSEQUENCE, STATED PLAINLY BECAUSE IT IS THE EXACT OUTCOME THE PARAGRAPHS ABOVE
        ARGUE AGAINST FOR THE ACQUISITION SIDE: every user in this group acquires
        Disposition_Deal_Actions at the next group recalculation, with no separate assignment
        step and no further gate. The nine disposition quick actions on Disposition_Record_Page,
        plus the disposition entries on LOI_Record_Page, NDA_Record_Page and
        Contract_Review_Record_Page, become visible and clickable for the whole population. That
        was accepted deliberately as a user decision; it is recorded here rather than left to be
        inferred from a member list that did not change.

        🔴 THE TWO SIDES ARE NOW ASYMMETRIC ON PURPOSE, AND MUST NOT BE "MADE CONSISTENT" IN
        EITHER DIRECTION WITHOUT A DECISION. Acquisitions keeps the separate directly-assigned
        driver set; dispositions no longer has one. Do not add Acquisition_Deal_Driver to this
        group to match the disposition side, and do not recreate a disposition driver set to
        match the acquisition side. See
        customPermissions/Disposition_Deal_Actions.customPermission-meta.xml, where the argument
        against this widening is corrected in place rather than deleted, and
        permissionsets/DPEG_Disposition_Edit.permissionset-meta.xml for the grant itself.

        ⚠ NO MEMBER OF THIS GROUP CHANGED ON 2026-08-19. The permissionSets list below is
        byte-identical to its previous state; this edit is comment-only. That matters because a
        PermissionSetGroup deploy REPLACES its member list wholesale, so an accidental edit here
        silently revokes access. The widening above arrives through an existing member's new
        grant, which is precisely why it is invisible in a diff of this file and has to be
        written down in it.

        SharePoint_Integration_Access joined this group on 2026-08-11 (SharePoint deal-folder
        feature, design D1-A/scoped-persona user decision) and THIS GROUP ONLY. The user
        deliberately declined DPEG_Transaction_Team even though the design doc's default
        recommendation lists both persona groups - do not "correct" this by adding it there.
        Membership was reconciled against the org before this edit; the deployed group carried no
        member absent from this file (see ARCHITECTURE.md §2's 2026-08-10 finding on this exact
        hazard - a PermissionSetGroup deploy replaces its member list wholesale).
    -->
    <permissionSets>DPEG_Acquisition_Edit</permissionSets>
    <permissionSets>DPEG_Apex_Access</permissionSets>
    <permissionSets>DPEG_App_Acquisition</permissionSets>
    <permissionSets>DPEG_App_Disposition</permissionSets>
    <permissionSets>DPEG_App_Transaction</permissionSets>
    <permissionSets>DPEG_Base_Access</permissionSets>
    <permissionSets>DPEG_Contact_Edit</permissionSets>
    <permissionSets>DPEG_Disposition_Edit</permissionSets>
    <permissionSets>DPEG_PropertyMgmt_View</permissionSets>
    <permissionSets>DPEG_TaskChecklist_View</permissionSets>
    <permissionSets>DPEG_Transaction_View</permissionSets>
    <permissionSets>Opportunity_Stage_Actions_Access</permissionSets>
    <permissionSets>SharePoint_Integration_Access</permissionSets>
</PermissionSetGroup>
